Never let CallbackMaxSizeEnforcer loop on a block size of 0.

AudioDeviceManager wraps its callback in CallbackMaxSizeEnforcer, which
splits each incoming block into pieces no larger than the size the device
reported in audioDeviceAboutToStart. A device that reported 0 (PipeWire's
JACK does, between jack_activate and its first cycle) makes that loop advance
by 0 samples per iteration, forever, on the audio thread - the whole process
is then a spinning core with no sound, and closing the device under it
crashes. The JACK device is fixed to never report 0 (juce-jack-buffer-size);
this is the guard for the next backend that does. A block larger than the
callback expected is the lesser harm, so it is passed through whole.
diff --git a/modules/juce_audio_devices/audio_io/juce_AudioDeviceManager.cpp b/modules/juce_audio_devices/audio_io/juce_AudioDeviceManager.cpp
--- a/modules/juce_audio_devices/audio_io/juce_AudioDeviceManager.cpp
+++ b/modules/juce_audio_devices/audio_io/juce_AudioDeviceManager.cpp
@@ -115,7 +115,13 @@ public:
 
         while (position < numSamples)
         {
-            const auto blockLength = jmin (maximumSize, numSamples - position);
+            // PLECTRIFY_NO_ZERO_BLOCK_SPIN: a device that reported a buffer size
+            // of 0 in audioDeviceAboutToStart (PipeWire's JACK does, between
+            // jack_activate and its first cycle) made this loop advance by 0
+            // forever, on the audio thread. A block larger than expected is
+            // the lesser harm; pass it through whole.
+            const auto blockLength = maximumSize > 0 ? jmin (maximumSize, numSamples - position)
+                                                     : numSamples - position;
 
             const auto addOffset = [position] (auto ptr) { return ptr + position; };
             std::transform (inputChannelData,  inputChannelData  + numInputChannels,  storedInputChannels .begin(), addOffset);
